sec.p:
This framing is advantageous for three reasons: first, the model yields a relatively small number of parameters that are both identifiable (i.e. are intuitive by eye) and interpretable (i.e. have biological meaning). This minimises overfitting and, as a result, makes the model robust to sample-to-sample variation. Secondly, it enables comparability across responses which involve very different mechanisms and timeframes. Thirdly, the same framework can be applied to any induced phenotype that can be measured quantitatively over time, such as gene expression, hormone accumulation, enzymatic conversions, volatile emissions and resistance induction, even if they show starkly different dynamics and biosynthetic processes, as they all typically exhibit the same behaviour: induction delay following stimulation and a rapid increase in response, which, after reaching a peak, gradually declines (1, 2, 28, 49–57). This is mathematically intuitive as these responses are built from a sequential chain (or combinatory network) of activation, synthesis, transport and eventually measurement/detection. Each step adds stochastic waiting time with their sum naturally producing a skewed, unimodal distribution (58). Modelling this kind of process usually involves treating each biochemical step as a stochastic waiting time drawn from a defined probability distribution and then combining these into a final waiting-time distribution which captures the total observed response dynamics. As such, we used a reparametrised gamma distribution, which retains its form when steps are combined and can thus describe both early and later phases of the response in a consistent way (see materials and methods for full model details):
R(t)=Rpeak⋅(t−tonsettpeak−tonset)(tmean−tonsettmean−tpeak−1)⋅exp⁡(−(t−tpeak)tmean−tpeak) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle R(t)=R_{\text {peak }} \cdot\left(\frac{t-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}\right)^{\left(\frac{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}-1\right)} \cdot \exp \left(\frac{-\left(t-t_{\text {peak }}\right)}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}\right)$$\end{document}
where:

Total response (Integral) – The integral is often difficult to measure experimentally; due to time limitations in the measurement processes, entire curves are not resolved and thus incomplete integrations are used (59). When fit onto the experimental data, our model can be used to calculate the theoretical integral of the response curve even if it is incomplete:
Integral=Rpeak(tmean−tpeak)1+tpeak−tonsettmean−tpeaketpeak−tonsettmean−tpeak(tpeak−tonset)−tpeak−tonsettmean−tpeakΓ(1+tpeak−tonsettmean−tpeak) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{Integral} =R_{\text {peak }}\left(t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}\right)^{1+\frac{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}} e^{\frac{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}}\left(t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}\right)^{-\frac{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}} \Gamma\left(1+\frac{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}\right)$$\end{document}
where Γ() is the Gamma function, a continuous generalisation of the factorial (46, 47).

For response dynamics, response length is typically defined as ‘broadness’, for example as full width at half max (FWHM) (6). The FWHM of the Gamma function has no analytical solution and must be determined numerically (62). Additionally, FWHM, does not explicitly capture the start of the response. For these reasons Duration is used as a simple alternative, achieving a similar descriptive value and used as a normalisation value in the ‘shape’ feature below.


Duration=tmean−tonset
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle Duration =t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {onset }}$$\end{document}

The ‘shape’ of a response curve is an abstract feature. Since our model is intended to be used over variable time scales, we developed a time-normalised (by Duration) metric that allows for comparison of curve shapes independently from response length. Further, we aimed to understand the symmetry of the curve in response to different stimuli or across plant genotypes/species to broadly assess temporal nuances. Shape presents a new phenotypic axis which may link to important biochemical or physiochemical properties that regulate induced responses. Shape is a normalised value, and when responses follow gamma distributions it falls between zero and one; a Shape of zero indicates a perfectly symmetrical curve (tmean = tpeak) and a shape of one represents a completely right-skewed curve (tpeak= tonset):
Shape=tmean−tpeaktmean−tonset \begin{document}$$\displaystyle Shape =\frac{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {onset }}}$$\end{document}
Taken together, the fit and derived parameters give a framework for comparing curves, particularly those that appear distinct by eye but are difficult to distinguish quantitatively (Fig 1B).

To model the waiting time probability distribution, we used a Gamma distribution:
f(t;α,β)=tα−1e−t/ββαΓ(α),t≥0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle f(t ; \alpha, \beta)=\frac{t^{\alpha-1} e^{-t / \beta}}{\beta^{\alpha} \Gamma(\alpha)}, \quad t \geq 0$$\end{document}
Where:

We begin with the standard Gamma distribution and introduce a shifted time variable tadj to account for a delay in the onset of the curve:
tadj=t−tonset \begin{document}$$\displaystyle t_{\text {adj }}=t-t_{\text {onset }}$$\end{document}
The Gamma probability density function is then given by:
f(t;α,β,tonset)=tadjα−1e−tadj/ββαΓ(α),t≥tonset \begin{document}$$\displaystyle f\left(t ; \alpha, \beta, t_{\text {onset }}\right)=\frac{t_{\text {adj }}^{\alpha-1} e^{-t_{\text {adj }} / \beta}}{\beta^{\alpha} \Gamma(\alpha)}, \quad t \geq t_{\text {onset }}$$\end{document}
Our next step is to relate the variables to observable quantities, and so we want to express α and β in terms of measurable features of the response curve.

In order to solve for α and β for these observable features we: Subtract the first from the second:


Mode-Mean=αβ−(α−1)β=(tmean−tonset)−(tpeak−tonset)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle Mode - Mean =\alpha \beta-(\alpha-1) \beta=\left(t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {onset }}\right)-\left(t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}\right)$$\end{document}



Simplify:


β=tmean−tpeak
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \beta=t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}$$\end{document}



Substitute into αβ = tmean − tonset:


α=tmean−tonsetβ=tmean−tonsettmean−tpeak
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \alpha=\frac{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{\beta}=\frac{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}$$\end{document}



The Gamma probability density function is normalised to integrate to 1.

However, in experimental data the absolute amplitude (the response intensity, Rpeak) is an observable quantity and so we define the response model:
R(t)=Rpeak⋅f(t;α,β,tonset)f(tpeak;α,β,tonset) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle R(t)=R_{\text {peak }} \cdot \frac{f\left(t ; \alpha, \beta, t_{\text {onset }}\right)}{f\left(t_{\text {peak }} ; \alpha, \beta, t_{\text {onset }}\right)}$$\end{document}
where Rpeak is the observed response at tpeak.

Substitute t = tpeak into f(t; α, β, tonset):


f(tpeak;α,β,tonset)=(tpeak−tonset)α−1exp⁡(−tpeak−tonsetβ)βαΓ(α)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle f\left(t_{\text {peak }} ; \alpha, \beta, t_{\text {onset }}\right)=\frac{\left(t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}\right)^{\alpha-1} \exp \left(-\frac{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{\beta}\right)}{\beta^{\alpha} \Gamma(\alpha)}$$\end{document}



Replace tpeak − tonset with (α − 1)β using the mode definition:


f(tpeak;α,β,tonset)=[(α−1)β]α−1exp⁡(−(α−1)ββ)βαΓ(α)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle f\left(t_{\text {peak }} ; \alpha, \beta, t_{\text {onset }}\right)=\frac{[(\alpha-1) \beta]^{\alpha-1} \exp \left(-\frac{(\alpha-1) \beta}{\beta}\right)}{\beta^{\alpha} \Gamma(\alpha)}$$\end{document}



Simplify the exponent:


exp⁡(−(α−1)ββ)=e−(α−1)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \exp \left(-\frac{(\alpha-1) \beta}{\beta}\right)=e^{-(\alpha-1)}$$\end{document}



Final expression:


f(tpeak;α,β,tonset)=[(α−1)β]α−1e−(α−1)βαΓ(α)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle f\left(t_{\text {peak }} ; \alpha, \beta, t_{\text {onset }}\right)=\frac{[(\alpha-1) \beta]^{\alpha-1} e^{-(\alpha-1)}}{\beta^{\alpha} \Gamma(\alpha)}$$\end{document}



We can therefore define:


Rpeak=f(tpeak;α,β,tonset)=[(α−1)β]α−1e−(α−1)βαΓ(α)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle R_{\text {peak }}=f\left(t_{\text {peak }} ; \alpha, \beta, t_{\text {onset }}\right)=\frac{[(\alpha-1) \beta]^{\alpha-1} e^{-(\alpha-1)}}{\beta^{\alpha} \Gamma(\alpha)}$$\end{document}



Substitute f(t)and (f-tpeak). into the ratio:


R(t)=Rpeak⋅(t−tonset)α−1exp⁡(−t−tonsetβ)βαΓ(α)(tpeak−tonset)α−1exp⁡(−tpeak−tonsetβ)βαΓ(α)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle R(t)=R_{\text {peak }} \cdot \frac{\frac{\left(t-t_{\text {onset }}\right)^{\alpha-1} \exp \left(-\frac{t-t_{\text {onset }}}{\beta}\right)}{\beta^{\alpha} \Gamma(\alpha)}}{\frac{\left(t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}\right)^{\alpha-1} \exp \left(-\frac{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}{\beta}\right)}{\beta^{\alpha} \Gamma(\alpha)}}$$\end{document}



Cancel the common terms β.Γ(α):


R(t)=Rpeak⋅(t−tonsettpeak−tonset)α−1⋅exp⁡(−(t−tonset)−(tpeak−tonset)β)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle R(t)=R_{\text {peak }} \cdot\left(\frac{t-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}\right)^{\alpha-1} \cdot \exp \left(-\frac{\left(t-t_{\text {onset }}\right)-\left(t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}\right)}{\beta}\right)$$\end{document}



Simplify the exponent:


R(t)=Rpeak⋅(t−tonsettpeak−tonset)α−1⋅exp⁡(−t−tpeakβ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle R(t)=R_{\text {peak }} \cdot\left(\frac{t-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}\right)^{\alpha-1} \cdot \exp \left(-\frac{t-t_{\text {peak }}}{\beta}\right)$$\end{document}



Replace α and β with expressions in terms of (tonset, tpeak, tmean) to give the final model:


R(t)=Rpeak⋅(t−tonsettpeak−tonset)tmean−tonset−1tmean−tpeak⋅exp⁡(−t−tpeaktmean−tpeak)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle R(t)=R_{\text {peak }} \cdot\left(\frac{t-t_{\text {onset }}}{t_{\text {peak }}-t_{\text {onset }}}\right)^{\frac{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {onset }}-1}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}} \cdot \exp \left(-\frac{t-t_{\text {peak }}}{t_{\text {mean }}-t_{\text {peak }}}\right)$$\end{document}



Where:
